let __v_871 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"air fryer\",\"airfryer\",\"air-fryer\"]},\"Unplug it and let it cool: smoke from an air fryer is nearly always fat or a bit of food on the element or in the base of the drawer, so wash the basket and the inside of the drawer properly and check the element has no crust burnt onto it. If it sparked, or the coating on the element is flaking, stop using it - a new drawer or a new machine is cheaper than a kitchen fire.\",\"Unplug it, check the fuse in the plug and try another socket; if the display stays dark the element or the thermal cut-out has gone and it is not a repair worth doing. If it lights up but never gets hot, the element itself has failed.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"slow cooker\",\"pressure cooker\",\"instant pot\",\"multi cooker\"]},\"Unplug it and stop using it: a slow cooker that smokes or smells hot has usually had its element seal go, and the outside being discoloured says the same thing. It is not a repair worth doing on a machine that costs what they cost.\",\"Check the socket and the fuse first, then that the pot or the inner bowl is sitting right down on the base - a slow cooker that will not heat is usually one of those three and not the machine.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"microwave\"]},\"Stop it and unplug it. Sparks in a microwave are metal - foil, a gold rim, a twist of cling film with wire in it - or a burnt waveguide cover, or charred food on the inside wall. Take the plate out, clean the walls and try a mug of water: if it still sparks with nothing metal inside, the cover or the magnetron has gone and it needs an engineer or replacing.\",\"If the light and the clock work but nothing heats, the magnetron or its fuse has gone and it is an engineer's job. If it is completely dead, check the fuse in the plug first - a microwave on the same ring as a kettle blows it more often than you would think.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"kettle\"]},\"Unplug it and stop using it: a kettle that sparks or smells of burning at the base has a fault in the element or the connector, and a kettle is cheaper than the risk.\",\"If it will not switch on at all, check the fuse in the plug and try another socket first, because the contacts in the base are a common fault. If it stays dead it is a new kettle rather than a repair.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"toaster\",\"sandwich toaster\",\"grill\",\"griddle\",\"waffle maker\",\"panini\"]},\"Unplug it and tip the crumbs out: a toaster that sparks or smells of burning usually has crumbs or a damaged element catching. If the element glows unevenly or a bit of it is missing, it is a new toaster rather than a repair.\",\"If it will not pop up, the spring or the timer has gone and it is a new toaster. If it will not heat at all, check the fuse in the plug first, then the timer dial.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bread maker\",\"bread machine\",\"stand mixer\",\"blender\",\"food processor\",\"soup maker\",\"yogurt maker\",\"coffee machine\",\"rice cooker\",\"deep fat fryer\",\"fryer\",\"juicer\"]},\"Unplug it and stop: a machine that smells of burning has cooked its motor, and the thermal cut-out that should have saved it has already done what it can. Motors and seals are the parts that fail and neither is worth paying someone to fit on a kitchen machine.\",\"Check the fuse in the plug, then the switch, then the lid or the bowl interlock - a machine that will not start very often thinks the lid is open. If it hums and does not turn, the motor or the belt has gone.\"]}]}")?;
