let __v_1284 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"oven\",\"An oven door that will not shut is almost always the hinge, not the door: open it wide and look for a bent or snapped hinge arm, and check nothing is jamming the seal or the shelf lip. A hinge arm is a cheap part - quote the oven's model number, and it is usually two screws to change.\"],[\"cupboard\",\"A cupboard door hanging off has pulled its hinge screws out of the board: take the door off, fill the holes and screw the hinge back where it was. If the hinge plate is bent, a new pair is a few pounds.\"],[\"catch\",\"A cupboard door that will not stay shut is the catch, not the hinges: a magnetic or roller catch lives on the inside edge, one half on the door and one on the carcass. Shut it and see whether the two halves touch - if they miss, slacken the screws and move the plate a millimetre, or fit a new catch for a couple of pounds.\"],[\"crooked\",\"A door that hangs crooked is set on the hinge, not by eye: the two screws on the cabinet side of each hinge plate move the door up and down and in and out. Slacken them a quarter turn, nudge the door, tighten, and try it closed; the screw nearest the door edge sets the gap.\"],[\"tap\",\"A tap that rocks is loose on the sink, not broken: go underneath and tighten the back nut or the clamp that holds the body to the worktop, and if the whole tap turns look for the small grub screw behind the base that locks it. A tap that drips is a washer or cartridge, which is a different job.\"],[\"shelf\",\"A snapped shelf is a new board, not a repair: measure the width, the depth and the thickness (15 or 18 mm is usual) and ask a timber yard or DIY shop to cut one to size - a few pounds, or ask for a white melamine shelf the same depth. If the pin holes have crumbled, fit a shelf support bracket instead of trusting the pins.\"],[\"drawer\",\"Drawer runners are a part you replace, not mend: pull the drawer right out and measure the length of the runner, and whether it fits on the side or underneath. Buy the pair, sold by length and by side or under, and swap them - two screws at the back and two at the front of each side.\"],[\"window\",\"A window that will not shut is usually out of line, not broken: check nothing is jamming the frame - a blind, a latch, a stone chip - then turn the handle and see whether the bar in the sash edge lines up with the keep on the frame. Lift or push the sash across to meet it. A handle that spins means a broken spindle, and that is a window fitter.\"]]}")?;
