let __v_2502 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"bearing\",\"A rumble or a grinding that gets louder through the spin, with black streaks round the door glass, is the drum bearing rather than the belt. Unplug it and turn the drum by hand: a gritty scrape, or a drum you can lift at the front edge, is the bearing. The part is only 10 to 20 pounds, but the drum comes out to fit it and on most machines the outer tub is sealed, so it is a two to four hour job for someone who has done one and a repairer will usually quote 100 to 200 pounds. On a machine more than six or seven years old that is more than the machine is worth, so replace it, and buy the next one with a five-year guarantee.\"],[\"belt\",\"A drive belt is a rubber loop round the motor and the drum, or round the motor and the brush bar. Unplug it and take the back panel off - on most machines that is a handful of screws - and look at the belt before buying anything: a snapped one is obvious, and one that has slipped off usually looks fine and is sitting loose at the bottom. Photograph the route it takes round the pulleys, because the path is the part people get wrong. Buy the belt for your model: the rating plate is inside the door or on the back, and a parts shop matches the code for about 5 to 10 pounds. Ten minutes with a screwdriver, half an hour on a machine where the back is tight. If it is under guarantee, ring the retailer instead, because opening it yourself can void it.\"],[\"pump\",\"The pump is the small motor at the bottom behind the kick plate, and it is the part that sends the water out. If the machine will not drain, empty it first through the little filter hose or the drain filter, then check the pump for hair, coins and bra wires before you buy a new one - a blocked impeller feels exactly like a dead pump. A pump for most washing machines and dishwashers is 15 to 30 pounds and comes out with two or three screws and a clip on the hoses; put a towel down, there is always water left in it.\"],[\"brush bar\",\"The brush bar is turned by a small belt from the motor, so the order is: unplug it, turn the machine over and cut the hair off the bar with scissors, check the belt has not slipped or snapped, then press the reset button on the top if it has one. If the bar turns freely by hand but not when it is switched on, the belt is the usual answer; if it will not turn by hand at all, a thread or a sock is jammed round the end of it. The belt is a few pounds for your model.\"]]}")?;
