let __v_4140 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ground rent\",\"ground rents\"]},\"Ground rent is the yearly charge a leaseholder pays to the freeholder for the land the flat stands on - often £50 to £250, though some newer leases set it much higher. Read the lease for how it reviews: one that doubles every ten years, or rises with the retail prices index, is a real problem, because a mortgage lender will refuse it and a buyer will too. Ask the seller for the last three years of demands.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"service charge\",\"service charges\",\"maintenance charge\",\"maintenance charges\",\"management fee\",\"management fees\",\"estate charge\",\"estate charges\"]},\"A service charge is your share of what the block costs to run: buildings insurance, the cleaner, the lift, the lights, the roof, and the managing agent's fee. It is not profit for the landlord - you are entitled to the yearly accounts and to challenge anything unreasonable at a tribunal - so ask for the last three years of it and what the reserve fund holds for big jobs. A charge that jumps in one year is usually a repair or an insurance renewal, and the accounts will say which.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"managing agent\",\"managing agents\",\"management company\",\"managing company\"]},\"The managing agent is the company the freeholder pays to run the block day to day: repairs, insurance, the service charge accounts and the cleaning. It is not the freeholder and it cannot sell you the freehold, but it is who you ring about the broken door. Ask for the agent's name and the last set of accounts before you buy, and put a complaint about the block in writing to the agent first, because the freeholder is only reachable through it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"freeholder\",\"freeholders\"]},\"The freeholder owns the building and the land; a leaseholder owns the right to live in one flat for a set number of years. The freeholder's jobs are the structure and the outside, paid for by the service charge, and the leaseholders have the right to buy that freehold between them if enough of them want to.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"freehold\"]},\"Freehold means you own the building and the land it stands on outright, with no end date and no ground rent or service charge to anyone. A house is usually freehold; a flat is usually leasehold, or the flat is leasehold and the freehold is owned by a company the flat owners share.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"leasehold\"]},\"Leasehold means you own the right to live in the property for the number of years left on the lease, not the land under it. You pay ground rent and a service charge to the freeholder, and the lease is the contract that says what you may and may not do - which is why the years left matter so much when you sell.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"covenant\",\"covenants\",\"deed of covenant\",\"deeds of covenant\",\"restrictive covenant\",\"restrictive covenants\"]},\"A covenant is a promise written into a deed. In a lease it is what you must and must not do (no subletting, keep the hallway clear); on a freehold it is usually a restriction on the land, such as not running a business from the house, and it can still be enforced fifty years on. A deed of covenant on its own is also the name for a signed promise to pay a fixed sum - often to a charity, so it can claim the tax back.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"easement\",\"easements\"]},\"An easement is a right to use someone else's land for one purpose: a right of way across a drive, drains running under a neighbour's garden, or access to lay pipes. It stays with the property rather than the person, so it is on the title and a buyer's solicitor will find it. You cannot build over one, and it needs registering to be safe.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"title deed\",\"title deeds\",\"title register\",\"land registry\"]},\"The title deeds are the documents that prove who owns a property. In England and Wales they are now held electronically by HM Land Registry, so there is nothing for you to keep in a safe - a copy of the title register can be downloaded for a few pounds. It lists the owner, the price last paid, the boundaries on a map and anything registered against the property, such as a mortgage or a covenant.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"conveyancer\",\"conveyancers\",\"conveyancing\"]},\"A conveyancer is the solicitor or licensed conveyancer who does the legal work of buying or selling a home: searches, the contract, the lease or title, the mortgage conditions and the money on completion. Fees are usually £800 to £2,000 plus searches and VAT; ask for the total quoted for your property, and use one who is on your lender's panel if you have a mortgage.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"what is a lease extension\",\"whats a lease extension\",\"lease extension mean\",\"lease extension meaning\",\"what does lease extension mean\",\"define lease extension\"]},\"A lease extension is a new, longer lease on the same flat: you pay the freeholder a premium and the years are added, 90 on top of what is left if you use the legal right, which you have once you have owned the flat for two years. Your solicitor serves the notice and the new lease is registered at the Land Registry; the premium is the negotiation. Do it before the lease drops under about 80 years, because the price climbs sharply after that.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lease extension\",\"extend my lease\",\"extend the lease\",\"extending my lease\",\"short lease\",\"lease length\",\"how long is the lease\",\"how long is my lease\",\"how long is a lease\",\"how long a lease\",\"how many years left on the lease\",\"how many years left on my lease\",\"years left on the lease\",\"years are left on the lease\",\"years are left on my lease\"]},\"A lease is your right to live there for a fixed number of years, and the length is what matters at the end: below about 80 years it gets expensive to extend and some lenders will not touch it. After two years of owning it you have the right to add 90 years at a peppercorn rent, which is a legal route rather than a favour, so a short lease is a negotiating point on the price and not a reason to walk away. Get the premium quoted by a surveyor who does lease extensions before you agree anything.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"what is a lease\",\"whats a lease\",\"what is a leasehold\",\"leasehold mean\",\"leasehold meaning\"]},\"A lease is the document that gives you the right to live in a flat for a set number of years - 99, 125 and 999 are the usual lengths - and it comes with ground rent, a service charge and rules about the building. You own the inside of the flat and the right to live there; the freeholder owns the structure and the land. The two numbers to check are how many years are left and what the ground rent does.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"epc\",\"energy performance certificate\",\"energy performance\"]},\"An EPC is the energy rating for the property, A to G, and it has to be done before a home is sold or rented, and it lasts ten years. E is the lowest rating a landlord may let a home on in England and Wales, so a flat rated F or G is either a bargaining point or a bill for insulation and a new boiler. Read the recommendation list on page three, because that is where the cheap wins are.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"gazumping\",\"gazumped\",\"gazumping mean\"]},\"Gazumping is a seller accepting a higher offer after they had already accepted yours, which in England and Wales is legal until contracts are exchanged - nothing is binding before that, and you can lose the survey and legal fees you have paid. The defences are to ask for the property to be taken off the market in writing and to move fast towards exchange; in Scotland the offer is binding much earlier, so the risk is different there.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"snagging list\",\"snagging\",\"snags list\"]},\"A snagging list is the list of small faults in a new build - a door that rubs, a crooked tile, a scratch on the worktop - which you hand to the developer to put right. Do it in the first days after you get the keys rather than at the end of the year, with a photo of each snag and where it is, and keep the emailed copy: the warranty usually gives you two years for this kind of thing, and the list is what starts the clock.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"peppercorn\",\"peppercorn rent\"]},\"A peppercorn rent is a ground rent of nothing at all - the name is the old token payment, and it means the lease has no ground rent to pay. A lease extended the statutory way ends up on one, so a peppercorn ground rent in a lease is a good sign rather than a puzzle.\"]}]}")?;
